  "worked_solution": "The reference angle is the acute angle that $\\theta $ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we create a right-triangle by drawing a vertical line from $P$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14225277/assets/6fdb497f69777c364f464e0ebd7ac4d4884679a3a38956da2818866d886614d2.png)\n\nSo, we have the following triangle, where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle of $\\theta .$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14225277/assets/9862a9d72f39e7f6d9abd861e4c3078cb67962d50548fae8ee98a634f262fed6.png)\n\nWe know that the hypotenuse has a length of $1$ because it is a radius of the unit circle. \n\nNow, using the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{0.64}{1}=0.64.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ using the inverse sine:\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(0.64)≈{40}^{∘}\n$$",
